  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Where a lambda's methods have been substituted with SubstituteMethods before it is observed, the parts it reads which read nothing of the argument are now shared across its observations as they are when it is not substituted. Substitution casts what a call is made on to the type declaring the substitution, and before this the analyzer took a cast of something captured for a part which can change and shared it, which made any call over it whose value is disposed of ineligible to be held and so left the whole lambda sharing nothing: a clock-built window was computed again by every element on every tick, as in 7.1.7. A cast which invokes nothing and keeps the object it casts is now decided on as what it casts is, and a shared part is as invariant to the analyzer as what it stands for, so a value made once from captured things and given to such a call is shared too. For a filtered query of 10,000 elements whose predicate was substituted and compares each element against a window computed from a clock, a tick which leaves the window where it was went from 287.4 to 0.03 μs, and a tick which moves it costs 0.82x what it did; each evaluation of the shared window allocates its value boxed, 32 bytes, where a tick allocated nothing before. SubstituteMethods no longer casts the arguments of a call, which it could always take as they were, nor what a call is made on where the method is already callable on it.\nWhich parts of a lambda are shared across its observations is written to the observer's logger at Debug level when the lambda is first observed (Epiforge_Extensions_Expressions_SubexpressionsShared), as is a lambda found to have such parts which nonetheless shares none of them (Epiforge_Extensions_Expressions_SubexpressionsNotShared), so a consumer's test can confirm what is shared without counting reads.",
